  "answer_core": "Do not pick a dissertation path yet. Treat the next two weeks as a bounded, low‑cost test of whether the Silva/data direction is actually real and finishable. Use two gates.\n\nGate 1: Data viability. Before any commitment, get concrete answers from Dr. Silva about what data exists, who controls access, when you could inspect sample files, what collaboration depth is expected, and what portion of the project would clearly be your dissertation. Do this after first speaking with your advisor so you are aligned on what you are probing for and what constraints your advisor cares about.\n\nGate 2: Fallback integrity. Confirm with your advisor that the current lab‑based fallback remains fully available, resourced, technically ready, and acceptable as a dissertation if the Silva path fails. Set a visible stop‑date and conditions under which you would stop pursuing Silva and return to the fallback.\n\nSequence the actors cleanly: talk to your advisor first, then run the Silva probe, and bring your committee in only once you know whether the data path is feasible and whether your advisor would support a pivot. Do not run multiple dissertation directions in parallel. Make the actual choice only after both gates have concrete evidence.",
